data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Determining External Validity**\n\nI'm now determining external validity, focusing on the ensemble's utility concerning unseen genres. I'm assessing its suitability for establishing the scope of claims related to the deviation from published law. It's also suitable for assessing internal-state monitoring’s incremental value compared to text monitoring outside of the original genres. I'm working on how this could serve as a terminal gate or solely as a scope test.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 1130,"totalTokenCount": 2295,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 1130}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 1165,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "mOsCa4etDrmsytMPq8bgwAo"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Defining Scope and Validity**\n\nI'm now zeroing in on defining scope and validity. I'm focusing on using an unseen-genre set for \"la portée de ce que l'équipe affirme\" to validate claims (i) and (ii), and also to falsify genre-specificity. I'm choosing a clearer stance: prioritizing assessing the scope (\"portée\") and not using it as an iterative gate, except as a terminal lock. This is because using unseen genres to drive cycles risks confounding selection bias and external validity, particularly under optional stopping. I am finalizing a falsifiable statement and measurement plan.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 1130,"totalTokenCount": 2587,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 1130}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 1457,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "mOsCa4etDrmsytMPq8bgwAo"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Evaluating Incremental Value**\n\nI'm now determining how best to measure the incremental value lost in internal state versus text on an unseen genre. I will measure the delta log-loss (or paired AUROC) and residue compared to published law on the unseen set, and I'll compare it to the development set. I'm recalling prior work, which seems very relevant, specifically Dwork et al. (2015), Recht et al. (2019), and clinical trial papers by Justice, Covinsky & Berlin (1999), Altman & Royston (2000), Pencina, D'Agostino et al. (2008), and Pepe et al. (2013). I'm focusing on ensuring accuracy in citations.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 1130,"totalTokenCount": 2857,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 1130}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 1727,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "mOsCa4etDrmsytMPq8bgwAo"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Confirming Literature Accuracy**\n\nI'm now verifying the exact titles and sources for some key references. I've confirmed Dwork et al. (2015), Justice et al. (1999), and Pepe et al. (2013) – or perhaps DeLong et al. (1988) – as rock solid. I've also verified Steyerberg & Harrell (2016). For Q1, this reference's insights on internal versus external validation are directly relevant to addressing the scope versus gate question. I'm noting objective control (time-stamped pre-registration).\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 1130,"totalTokenCount": 3092,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 1130}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 1962,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "mOsCa4etDrmsytMPq8bgwAo"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Analyzing Test Protocol**\n\nI'm now focusing on the test protocol, specifically the internal passage criterion, and how it can be compared using an automated paired permutation test between the internal data and the unseen set. I'm exploring when to consult this set. Consulting at every cycle is out, because it would hurt independence, but I'm intrigued by consulting it only once. Considering group sequential design or reusable holdout.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 1130,"totalTokenCount": 3361,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 1130}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 2231,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "mOsCa4etDrmsytMPq8bgwAo"}

